        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "1b41226c-8c4c-577d-9713-6f46b77b768d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Charles White Whittlesey\n- Organizations: United States Army, 326th Infantry Regiment, 82nd Division, Allied forces\n- Events: Meuse-Argonne Offensive, World War I\n- Concepts: Lost Battalion, Medal of Honor\n\nFacts:\n- During World War I, the Meuse-Argonne Offensive was a major part of the final Allied offensive that stretched along the entire Western Front.\n- In October 1918, Charles White Whittlesey, a United States Army officer and Medal of Honor recipient, led the Lost Battalion in the Meuse-Argonne Offensive during the final stages of World War I.\n- During World War I, the 326th Infantry Regiment served as a United States Army infantry regiment in the 82nd Division and fought in the Meuse-Argonne Offensive.\n- In 1919, the 326th Infantry Regiment was inactivated.\n- Charles White Whittlesey was born on January 20, 1884, and was presumed dead on November 26, 1921.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "b13b1112-1b8f-52cd-a2c2-d7c74008d0dc",
        "text": "Page: Charles White Whittlesey. Sentence: Lieutenant Colonel Charles White Whittlesey (January 20, 1884 – presumed dead November 26, 1921) was a United States Army officer and an American Medal of Honor recipient who led the \"Lost Battalion\" in the Meuse-Argonne Offensive in October 1918 during the final stages of World War I.\nPage: Meuse-Argonne Offensive. Sentence: The Meuse-Argonne Offensive, also known as the Maas-Argonne Offensive and the Battle of the Argonne Forest, was a major part of the final Allied offensive of World War I that stretched along the entire Western Front.\nPage: 326th Infantry Regiment (United States). Sentence: The 326th Infantry Regiment was an infantry regiment of the United States Army that saw active service during World War I, as part of the 82nd Division and fought in the Meuse-Argonne Offensive and was inactivated in 1919.",
